@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Octo 11: hypothesis tests)

Each test returns `h` (1 means reject at the 5 % level), the p-value and usually a confidence interval and statistics.

@section(t and z tests)

One sample against a value, two independent samples, or paired samples.

@octo
a = [5.1 4.9 5.6 5.8 6.0 5.4 5.3 5.7];
b = [4.8 4.6 5.0 5.2 5.1 4.7 4.9 5.0];
[h, p, ci] = ttest(a, 5)
[h, p] = ttest2(a, b)
[h, p] = ttest(a, b)
[h, p] = ztest(a, 5, 0.4)
@end

@section(Variance and goodness of fit)

Test equal variances before choosing a pooled t test; test normality before trusting one.

@octo
a = [5.1 4.9 5.6 5.8 6.0 5.4 5.3 5.7];
b = [4.8 4.6 5.0 5.2 5.1 4.7 4.9 5.0];
[h, p] = vartest2(a, b)
[h, p] = vartest(a, 0.1)
[h, p] = kstest(zscore(a))
[h, p] = lillietest(a)
[h, p] = jbtest(a)
[h, p] = chi2gof([18 25 31 26], "P", [0.2 0.3 0.3 0.2])
@end

@section(Rank tests)

When the data are not normal, compare ranks instead.

@octo
a = [5.1 4.9 5.6 5.8 6.0 5.4 5.3 5.7];
b = [4.8 4.6 5.0 5.2 5.1 4.7 4.9 5.0];
p = ranksum(a, b)
p = signrank(a - b)
p = kruskalwallis([a b], [ones(1, 8) 2*ones(1, 8)])
p = friedman([1 2 3; 2 3 4; 1 3 5; 2 2 4])
@end
